mode: "100644"
text: "# Competitor analysis 2026 — verified\n\n*Volatile. Re-verify quarterly (benchmarks and platform norms shift).*\n\n## The point (don't miss it)\nThe goal is **not to copy** rivals — it's to gather public intelligence and **carve out a space you can\nown.** \"Who is winning?\" matters far less than **\"why are they winning, and how do we do better?\"**\nOutput = your *differentiated* strategy, not a carbon copy.\n\n## Who to analyse (roster the right set)\n- **Direct** — sell a similar product / target the same audience.\n- **Aspirational** — brands you admire or that dominate the space.\n- **Adjacent / indirect** — analogous niches competing for the same attention, plus the incumbent\n  behaviour or \"do nothing\" you're displacing.\n- **~5–8 profiles** is the benchmarking sweet spot. **Match by industry, audience, and format** — a\n  B2B SaaS account vs a consumer-fashion brand teaches you nothing.\n\n## What to track (signals, not vanity)\nBenchmarking is **not tallying likes or followers.** Track: **engagement rate**, **share of voice\n(SOV)**, **follower-growth trend**, **posting cadence**, **content formats/mix**, **hooks & tone**,\n**visual style & CTAs**, **sentiment**, **paid vs organic mix** (via ad libraries), and **how content\nmaps to funnel stages** (awareness vs conversion). **SWOT** is the standard wrap-up frame.\n\n## Benchmarks (2026 — relative beats absolute; verify-quarterly)\nMedian **follower-based** engagement rates vary ~25× across platforms: **TikTok ~3.7%**, **Instagram\n~0.48%**, **Facebook ~0.15%**, **X ~0.12%** (Socialinsider 2026). So a \"2% on X\" looks weak but is\n~16× the median. **Watch the denominator:** LinkedIn's oft-quoted **~5%** is engagement *per\nimpression* (LinkedIn's native formula), and Buffer's 2026 benchmarks are *by reach* (LinkedIn ~6%,\nFacebook ~5.6%) — impression/reach-based numbers are NOT comparable to follower-based ones. Compute\nevery account in your set on **one formula**. **The useful benchmark is relative** — your trend vs\nyour *direct set* over time, not platform-wide averages.\n\n## The AI-era layer (new, important)\nAlso analyse competitors' **AI/LLM visibility** — how often they're surfaced/cited by ChatGPT,\nGemini, Claude — and their **GEO / AI-search** presence (ties to `ai-search-optimization`). Plus their\n**influencer/UGC partnerships** and **paid creative** (Meta Ad Library, TikTok Creative Center).\n\n## Pitfalls\nComparing unlike things · ignoring platform context · chasing platform averages instead of the direct\nset · vanity metrics over outcome-linked ones · one-off instead of a **monthly/quarterly** loop.\n\n## The hard limit — public data only\nCompetitor tools and native views surface **public** signals; their data is **shallower than your own\nanalytics** and partly **estimated.** You **cannot** see a rival's private analytics, revenue, or ROI\n— estimate ranges with stated uncertainty; **never fabricate** a competitor metric. No scraping\nprivate/non-public surfaces, no fake/sockpuppet monitoring accounts, no content theft.\n"
